WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.360
When something breaks in the cloud,
whose job was it?

00:00:03.585 --> 00:00:06.455
Four misunderstandings
cause most of the trouble.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:09.440
First, the provider is certified,

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:10.800
so we are secure.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.100
The certificate is real,
and it stops at a line.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.890
Below the line,
the provider is responsible.

00:00:17.385 --> 00:00:18.575
Above it, our data,

00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:21.415
our users and our configuration are ours.

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:25.445
In every service model,
identity and data stay with us.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.330
A clean provider audit can sit
next to our own open storage.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.980
Second, it is in the cloud,
so it is patched and backed up.

00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:36.495
The provider patches the physical host.

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:40.375
The operating system inside
our rented server is ours.

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.570
The provider's status page
shows green throughout.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.040
And so does a database
left open to the world.

00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:50.347
Third, the vendor takes
care of everything.

00:00:50.662 --> 00:00:51.692
With finished software,

00:00:52.222 --> 00:00:56.192
the vendor runs the application and
recovers it when their side fails.

00:00:56.582 --> 00:00:59.412
We own the users,
their rights and our own settings.

00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:01.847
When our administrator deletes a mailbox,

00:01:02.337 --> 00:01:03.727
that is not their failure.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.937
Their backup does not bring it back.

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:08.593
Fourth, it is in the contract.

00:01:09.028 --> 00:01:11.598
A service level agreement covers uptime.

00:01:11.828 --> 00:01:15.638
Breach notification may only say,
within a reasonable time.

00:01:15.863 --> 00:01:18.233
Then we read about our
own breach in the news.

00:01:18.563 --> 00:01:21.893
And our notification clock
started when we became aware.

00:01:22.983 --> 00:01:24.193
What good looks like.

00:01:25.258 --> 00:01:27.648
A responsibility matrix,
one page per service,

00:01:27.858 --> 00:01:30.088
signed by a named owner on our side.

00:01:30.343 --> 00:01:34.693
The provider's audit report lists our
controls, in the auditor's own words.

00:01:35.063 --> 00:01:36.593
Every workload has an owner.

00:01:36.953 --> 00:01:38.843
We back up our own software data.

00:01:39.193 --> 00:01:42.523
A contract checklist asks
for notification in hours.

00:01:42.913 --> 00:01:45.103
This is mostly paperwork and ownership.

00:01:45.553 --> 00:01:46.703
NIS2 is clear:

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.923
outsourcing a layer does not
outsource the obligation.

00:01:51.897 --> 00:01:53.107
A suggested first step.

00:01:53.392 --> 00:01:58.242
Ask for the responsibility matrix of
the five services we depend on most.

00:01:58.587 --> 00:02:02.097
And for the name of the person
on our side who signed each one.

00:02:02.297 --> 00:02:04.687
If either is missing, that is the finding.

00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:07.933
Four misunderstandings.

00:02:08.083 --> 00:02:11.813
The cloud is a division of labour,
and our part is written down.

